-- [Female Reproductive Anatomy] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A patient undergoes a uterine procedure that leaves scar tissue destroying the deepest layer of the endometrium in one region of the uterine wall, while the smooth muscle beneath it and the endometrial lining elsewhere remain intact. Over subsequent menstrual cycles, what is the most likely consequence for the endometrium in the scarred region specifically?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The lining there will thicken normally each cycle but will fail to shed during menstruation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The lining there will not regenerate after menstruation, leaving that region persistently thin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The lining there will shed continuously rather than only during the menstrual phase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The lining there will thicken excessively each cycle because shedding can no longer occur"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' The endometrium consists of two layers: the stratum basalis, which lies adjacent to the myometrium and does not shed during menses, and the overlying stratum functionalis, which thickens under estrogen and progesterone, provides the implantation site, and is the only layer lost at menstruation. Because the functionalis is shed each cycle, it must be rebuilt from the retained stratum basalis; once menstrual flow ceases, rising estrogen stimulates the lining to proliferate again from this basal layer. The "deepest layer of the endometrium" destroyed by scarring is therefore the stratum basalis, the regenerative source. (Choice B) is correct: with the stratum basalis destroyed, the scarred region loses the base from which the functionalis regrows, so after each menstrual shedding that region cannot rebuild its lining and remains persistently thin. (Choice A) is incorrect: it is the stratum functionalis, not the basalis, that sheds at menstruation; destroying the deep basalis removes the regenerative base rather than blocking shedding, so a normally thickened lining that simply fails to shed is not the expected outcome. (Choice C) is incorrect: shedding is triggered by the cyclic fall in progesterone acting on the functionalis, not by the presence of the basalis, so losing the basalis would not convert normal cyclic shedding into continuous shedding. (Choice D) is incorrect: excessive thickening would require an intact functionalis responding to hormones, but the scarred region has lost its regenerative base, so it cannot build up a thick lining at all, let alone an excessive one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the two-layer structure of the endometrium—identifying the destroyed deep layer as the non-shedding stratum basalis and reasoning that regeneration of the functionalis depends on it—to predict the outcome in a novel clinical scenario.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '5f223024-0f2b-4379-a6cb-bd7e43eae4cc';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '5f223024-0f2b-4379-a6cb-bd7e43eae4cc';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('5f223024-0f2b-4379-a6cb-bd7e43eae4cc', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses which layer sheds vs. regenerates'),
  ('5f223024-0f2b-4379-a6cb-bd7e43eae4cc', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributes shedding control to the wrong layer'),
  ('5f223024-0f2b-4379-a6cb-bd7e43eae4cc', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes lost shedding causes buildup, ignoring loss of regenerative base');

-- [Mitosis Phases] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During mitosis in an animal cell, one chromosome''s kinetochore repeatedly fails to attach to a spindle microtubule, while all of the other chromosomes have correctly aligned along the center of the spindle. What is the most likely immediate consequence for this cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The cell halts before separating its sister chromatids and does not advance to the next stage of mitosis."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cell separates all of its sister chromatids on schedule but produces two nuclei of unequal size."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cell reverses back into prophase so the nuclear envelope can re-form and repackage the chromatin."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cell proceeds through cytokinesis and then repairs the unattached chromosome in the resulting daughter cells."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' Mitosis is monitored by checkpoints, and the textbook explains that "a crucial stop and go checkpoint in metaphase ensures that the cell is fully prepared to complete cell division," specifically that "all sister chromatids are properly attached to their respective microtubules and lined up at the metaphase plate before the signal is given to separate them during anaphase." Because one kinetochore has not attached to a microtubule, the requirement to advance is not met, and the checkpoint withholds the go signal. (Choice A) is correct: with an unattached kinetochore the metaphase checkpoint is not satisfied, so the cell arrests and does not receive the signal to separate its sister chromatids or move into anaphase, guarding against unequal chromosome distribution. (Choice B) is incorrect: the purpose of the checkpoint is precisely to prevent separation until every chromatid is attached, so the cell does not proceed to split the chromatids on schedule; separation is blocked, not merely made unequal. (Choice C) is incorrect: the cell cycle does not run in reverse, and the checkpoint pauses forward progress rather than reconstituting an earlier phase such as prophase with a re-formed nuclear envelope. (Choice D) is incorrect: the checkpoint acts before anaphase to stop division, so the cell does not complete cytokinesis and then fix the error afterward in daughter cells; allowing an unattached chromosome to segregate is the outcome the checkpoint exists to prevent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the function of the metaphase (spindle-assembly) checkpoint to predict how a cell responds when one kinetochore fails to attach, rather than recall the name of a phase.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '79df7ad0-59c2-44a2-b3dc-d4017bb86086';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '79df7ad0-59c2-44a2-b3dc-d4017bb86086';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('79df7ad0-59c2-44a2-b3dc-d4017bb86086', 'B', 'misconception', 'checkpoint-allows-flawed-division'),
  ('79df7ad0-59c2-44a2-b3dc-d4017bb86086', 'C', 'misconception', 'cell-cycle-runs-in-reverse'),
  ('79df7ad0-59c2-44a2-b3dc-d4017bb86086', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'repair-after-division-instead-of-arrest-before');

-- [Male Reproductive Anatomy] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A young man presents with delayed puberty and small testes. Genetic testing reveals a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the receptor for luteinizing hormone (LH), so his LH receptors cannot respond to their ligand at all. His hypothalamus and anterior pituitary are structurally and functionally normal. Compared with an unaffected man, what is the most likely pattern of his serum testosterone and serum LH concentrations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Testosterone is decreased and LH is increased."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Testosterone is decreased and LH is decreased."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Testosterone is increased and LH is decreased."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Testosterone is normal and LH is normal."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' In the testis, LH binds to receptors on the interstitial (Leydig) cells and upregulates their production of testosterone, while the anterior pituitary''s release of LH is held in check by negative feedback: rising testosterone binds androgen receptors on the hypothalamus and pituitary to suppress GnRH and LH, and falling testosterone releases that brake so more LH is secreted. If the LH receptor cannot respond, Leydig cells receive no signal, so testosterone synthesis falls; the resulting low testosterone removes feedback inhibition, so the intact pituitary secretes more LH. (Choice A) is correct: with dead LH receptors the Leydig cells cannot make testosterone, so testosterone falls, and the loss of testosterone-mediated negative feedback drives the hypothalamic-pituitary axis to secrete more LH. (Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly predicts low testosterone but ignores negative feedback—low testosterone disinhibits the hypothalamus and pituitary, so LH rises rather than falls (a decreased-LH pattern would instead point to a hypothalamic or pituitary defect). (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the primary effect, since a nonfunctional LH receptor cannot stimulate Leydig cells to raise testosterone; without high testosterone there is also no feedback signal to lower LH. (Choice D) is incorrect: because the defect lies in the Leydig cells'' own LH receptor, testosterone production cannot be maintained at normal levels, so neither hormone stays normal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the LH-to-Leydig-cell signaling step together with the negative-feedback logic of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis to a novel loss-of-function scenario.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6219aa88-d5b3-4cc6-95eb-9cde88eb3162';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6219aa88-d5b3-4cc6-95eb-9cde88eb3162';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6219aa88-d5b3-4cc6-95eb-9cde88eb3162', 'B', 'misconception', 'ignores feedback loop / assumes both hormones move together'),
  ('6219aa88-d5b3-4cc6-95eb-9cde88eb3162', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'reverses direction of the primary hormone effect'),
  ('6219aa88-d5b3-4cc6-95eb-9cde88eb3162', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes compensation restores normal levels');

-- [Female Reproductive Anatomy] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During the late follicular phase, a single dominant follicle secretes far more estrogen than the entire earlier cohort of follicles did together, and plasma estrogen climbs to a sustained peak. Rather than suppressing the anterior pituitary as it did earlier in the cycle, this estrogen peak is immediately followed by a sharp burst of luteinizing hormone (LH) that triggers ovulation. Which change in the hypothalamic–pituitary response to estrogen best accounts for this outcome?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Sustained high estrogen switches the pituitary response to estrogen from negative feedback to positive feedback"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sustained high estrogen strengthens the negative feedback that estrogen exerts on the pituitary throughout the cycle"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Rising progesterone from the follicle overrides estrogen feedback and directly drives the pituitary to release LH"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A rapid fall in estrogen removes negative feedback and allows the pituitary to release stored LH"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' Through most of the ovarian cycle, estrogen from the growing follicles acts by negative feedback, restraining GnRH, LH, and FSH secretion. The textbook explains that once a single dominant follicle produces so much estrogen that plasma concentrations become extremely high, this "triggers a regulatory switch in the anterior pituitary that responds by secreting large amounts of LH and FSH," and that "the positive feedback loop by which more estrogen triggers release of more LH and FSH only occurs at this point in the cycle." This LH surge is what induces ovulation. (Choice A) is correct: the same high-estrogen signal that normally inhibits the pituitary instead flips to positive feedback when estrogen is sustained and very high, so more estrogen now drives more LH, producing the surge. (Choice B) is incorrect: if negative feedback simply strengthened, high estrogen would suppress LH further, and no surge could occur — this ignores the feedback switch the textbook describes. (Choice C) is incorrect: progesterone is secreted in large amounts only after ovulation, by the luteinized corpus luteum, and it drives negative feedback that keeps LH low; it is not the trigger of the pre-ovulatory LH surge. (Choice D) is incorrect: at the time of the surge estrogen is at a sustained peak, not falling, and it is the high estrogen — not withdrawal of estrogen — that provokes LH release. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept that a hormone''s feedback effect can reverse from negative to positive under sustained high concentrations to explain the pre-ovulatory LH surge.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '97deb245-66fa-4bda-a71f-d920ea6ca57a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '97deb245-66fa-4bda-a71f-d920ea6ca57a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('97deb245-66fa-4bda-a71f-d920ea6ca57a', 'B', 'misconception', 'estrogen always inhibits gonadotropins (negative feedback only)'),
  ('97deb245-66fa-4bda-a71f-d920ea6ca57a', 'C', 'misconception', 'progesterone drives the pre-ovulatory LH surge'),
  ('97deb245-66fa-4bda-a71f-d920ea6ca57a', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'falling estrogen (withdrawal) triggers the LH surge');

-- [Mitosis Phases] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a cell preparing to divide, the metaphase checkpoint normally halts the cycle until every chromosome''s two kinetochores are attached to microtubules extending from opposite poles. A researcher chemically silences this checkpoint. In one such cell, a single chromosome has both of its sister-chromatid kinetochores captured by microtubules from the same pole, yet the cell is allowed to proceed directly into anaphase. Assuming every other chromosome is attached and separates normally, what is the most likely distribution of that one chromosome''s genetic material to the two daughter cells?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Both sister chromatids of that chromosome are pulled to the same pole, giving one daughter an extra copy and the other daughter none."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The two sister chromatids of that chromosome separate normally, so each daughter still receives exactly one copy of it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The sister chromatids of that chromosome remain joined at the centromere and are excluded from both daughter nuclei."}, {"label": "D", "text": "One sister chromatid goes to each daughter, but both copies are delivered in a still-condensed state that blocks their later transcription."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' At anaphase, sister chromatids are separated and each is pulled toward the pole whose spindle microtubules are attached to its kinetochore; the metaphase checkpoint normally verifies that a chromosome''s two kinetochores are bound to opposite poles before separation is permitted (lines 5481-5484, 5527-5529). When both kinetochores of one chromosome are captured by the same pole and the checkpoint is silenced, the shortening microtubules drag both chromatids in the same direction. (Choice A) is correct: because both kinetochores point to one pole, anaphase pulls both sister chromatids toward that single pole, so one daughter cell inherits two copies of the chromosome and the other inherits zero—a nondisjunction-type mis-segregation that the checkpoint exists to prevent. (Choice B) is incorrect: normal one-copy-per-daughter segregation requires the two kinetochores to face opposite poles, which is exactly the condition that fails here, so proceeding into anaphase cannot yield an even split for this chromosome. (Choice C) is incorrect: anaphase separation reflects loss of centromeric cohesion, not the direction of attachment, so the chromatids do separate at the centromere; misattachment changes where they go, not whether they split. (Choice D) is incorrect: the condensation state of chromatin is governed by phase progression (chromosomes decondense in telophase, lines 5487-5489) and is independent of which pole a chromatid is pulled toward, so faulty pole attachment would not leave delivered chromosomes permanently condensed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the kinetochore-to-pole attachment mechanism to a checkpoint-failure scenario and predict how mis-oriented attachment redistributes chromosomes to the daughter cells.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'de02cc30-990c-4a7f-bab0-2ec89a179762';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'de02cc30-990c-4a7f-bab0-2ec89a179762';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('de02cc30-990c-4a7f-bab0-2ec89a179762', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes anaphase always segregates evenly regardless of attachment'),
  ('de02cc30-990c-4a7f-bab0-2ec89a179762', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses attachment error with failure of centromeric cohesion release'),
  ('de02cc30-990c-4a7f-bab0-2ec89a179762', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'imports condensation state as a consequence of mis-attachment');

-- [Oogenesis] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a natural cycle, a single egg cell is released from the ovary and swept into the oviduct, where it remains for roughly a day before it is cleared without ever encountering a sperm. Considering how female meiosis is timed, which description best fits the meiotic state of this released cell during the interval it spends waiting in the oviduct?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A secondary oocyte that has finished the first meiotic division but is halted partway through the second"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A primary oocyte that is still halted partway through the first meiotic division"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A mature ovum that has already completed both meiotic divisions before leaving the ovary"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A diploid precursor cell that has replicated its DNA but not yet begun the first meiotic division"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' Female meiosis is spread across a lifetime and paused at two checkpoints: a primary oocyte sits arrested in meiosis I from before birth, and only a pre-ovulatory luteinizing hormone surge triggers it to resume and complete meiosis I, yielding a secondary oocyte plus the first polar body. The textbook states that this secondary oocyte "eventually leaves the ovary during ovulation" and that "all secondary oocytes remain arrested in metaphase of meiosis II until fertilization," so the ovulated cell is caught between the two divisions until fertilization occurs. (Choice A) is correct: the released cell has already completed meiosis I (that is why the LH surge preceded ovulation) and is arrested midway through meiosis II at metaphase II, which "Only upon fertilization does the oocyte complete"; with no fertilization here, it stays in this second-division arrest and is cleared. (Choice B) is incorrect: although primary oocytes are indeed "arrested in this stage of meiosis I," that arrest is broken by the LH surge before ovulation, so the cell in the oviduct has already advanced past meiosis I and is no longer a primary oocyte. (Choice C) is incorrect: the ovary does not release a finished gamete, because the "single haploid ovum... is produced only if there is penetration of the secondary oocyte by a sperm cell," and no sperm is present. (Choice D) is incorrect: meiosis in this lineage began during fetal life, not at ovulation, so the ovulated cell is a haploid product of meiosis I, not a diploid precursor awaiting its first division. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the two-checkpoint timing of female meiosis to a specific physiological moment—an ovulated, unfertilized cell—rather than recall a gamete count.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0bfa26e0-39d7-44a1-9680-63f23a99fc3c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0bfa26e0-39d7-44a1-9680-63f23a99fc3c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0bfa26e0-39d7-44a1-9680-63f23a99fc3c', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-arrest-checkpoint'),
  ('0bfa26e0-39d7-44a1-9680-63f23a99fc3c', 'C', 'misconception', 'ovulation-releases-finished-ovum'),
  ('0bfa26e0-39d7-44a1-9680-63f23a99fc3c', 'D', 'misconception', 'meiosis-begins-at-ovulation');

-- [Female Reproductive Anatomy] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an excised human uterine tube, a researcher applies a drug that halts the beating of the ciliated cells lining the mucosa while leaving the tube''s smooth muscle able to contract. A freshly ovulated secondary oocyte is placed at the fimbriated end, and its progress toward the uterine (isthmus) end is tracked. Compared with an untreated tube, what is the MOST likely effect on the oocyte''s movement toward the uterus?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Movement toward the uterus is essentially unchanged, because the smooth-muscle contractions of the tube wall alone carry the oocyte."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Movement toward the uterus is impaired, because the uterus-directed current that the cilia normally generate is lost."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Movement toward the uterus is faster, because the cilia normally hold the oocyte back to allow time for fertilization."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Movement reverses back toward the fimbriated end, because the cilia normally set the direction of flow within the tube."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' A secondary oocyte lacks flagella and cannot swim, so its journey through the uterine tube depends entirely on forces supplied by the tube wall: coordinated ciliary beating that generates a current flowing toward the uterus, working together with estrogen-driven smooth-muscle contractions. Because the ciliary current is one of the two major propulsive forces, removing it substantially weakens transport rather than merely fine-tuning it. (Choice A) is incorrect: smooth-muscle contractions contribute to transport, but the textbook describes the cilia-generated current as critical to moving the oocyte, so losing it is not something muscle contraction fully compensates for. (Choice B) is correct: the cilia produce the current directed toward the uterus, so abolishing their beating removes a principal driver and impairs the oocyte''s progress. (Choice C) is incorrect: the cilia propel the oocyte toward the uterus rather than restraining it, so their loss slows transport instead of speeding it up. (Choice D) is incorrect: the cilia contribute the forward-directed force, but their loss removes propulsion; it does not create a reverse current that carries the oocyte back toward the fimbriae. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that oocyte transport depends on externally generated ciliary and muscular forces to predict the outcome of selectively eliminating the ciliary current.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6eca5f2f-804c-47c3-996d-f6eab4c5b3e0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6eca5f2f-804c-47c3-996d-f6eab4c5b3e0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6eca5f2f-804c-47c3-996d-f6eab4c5b3e0', 'A', 'misconception', 'over-crediting one mechanism (smooth-muscle peristalsis) and dismissing the ciliary current'),
  ('6eca5f2f-804c-47c3-996d-f6eab4c5b3e0', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'cilia imagined as anchoring/restraining the oocyte rather than propelling it'),
  ('6eca5f2f-804c-47c3-996d-f6eab4c5b3e0', 'D', 'misconception', 'believing loss of the ciliary current reverses flow rather than removing forward propulsion');

-- [Gamete Structure & Fertilization] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In an in vitro fertilization study, sperm from one donor swim normally and reach the oocyte, but they cannot digest a path through the zona pellucida, so fertilization never occurs. Analysis shows the sperm cannot release their stored digestive enzymes upon contact with the zona pellucida. A defect in which region of the sperm most directly accounts for this failure?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The acrosomal cap covering the head"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The compact nucleus within the head"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The mitochondria of the mid-piece"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The axial filament of the tail"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' A mature sperm is regionally specialized: the head carries the haploid nucleus capped by the acrosome, the mid-piece is packed with mitochondria, and the tail contains the flagellum built around an axial filament. Each region performs one job in fertilization, so a failure in a specific fertilization step points to the region responsible for that step. Here the sperm swim and reach the oocyte normally but cannot enzymatically clear the zona pellucida, which identifies the step that is broken. (Choice A) is correct: the acrosome is the cap that ''is filled with lysosomal enzymes important for preparing sperm to participate in fertilization''; during the acrosomal reaction it ''releases its stored digestive enzymes'' that ''clear a path through the zona pellucida.'' Sperm that cannot release these enzymes fail exactly at the described step, so a defective acrosomal cap most directly explains the result. (Choice B) is incorrect: the compact haploid nucleus stores and delivers the paternal DNA into the oocyte after fusion; a nuclear defect would corrupt the genetic contribution, not the enzymatic penetration of the zona pellucida. (Choice C) is incorrect: the mid-piece mitochondria generate the ATP that powers the flagellum, so their failure would impair swimming and motility — but the stem specifies the sperm swim normally and reach the oocyte, ruling this out. (Choice D) is incorrect: the axial filament is the microtubule core of the flagellum built from a centriole, providing the tail''s structural framework for beating; a defect there would again show up as a motility problem, not as an inability to digest the zona pellucida. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply each sperm region''s specialized function to a described fertilization failure and infer which structure is responsible.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd9d23647-d2b0-499e-a5fe-910870e3bd54';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd9d23647-d2b0-499e-a5fe-910870e3bd54';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d9d23647-d2b0-499e-a5fe-910870e3bd54', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'right-region-wrong-function'),
  ('d9d23647-d2b0-499e-a5fe-910870e3bd54', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'correct-fact-wrong-step'),
  ('d9d23647-d2b0-499e-a5fe-910870e3bd54', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'motility-structure-confusion');

-- [Loss of Cell-Cycle Control & Cancer] ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher engineers a cell line in which p53 is fully functional but the p21 protein has been knocked out entirely. The cells are then exposed to a dose of radiation that damages their DNA. Compared with wild-type cells, how are these p21-null cells MOST likely to behave at the G1 checkpoint?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "They arrest normally at G1, because p53 alone binds and inhibits the Cdk/cyclin complexes to stall the cycle."}, {"label": "B", "text": "They fail to sustain the G1 arrest, because p21 is the protein that inhibits Cdk/cyclin activity to enforce the halt."}, {"label": "C", "text": "They arrest permanently at G1, because losing p21 leaves p53 unopposed and locks the checkpoint shut."}, {"label": "D", "text": "They bypass G1 arrest, because p21 is the sensor that detects the DNA damage in the first place."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' At the G1 checkpoint the negative regulators act in a relay: p53 detects damaged DNA and initiates the response, but the actual braking is carried out downstream. As the text explains, "As p53 levels rise, the production of p21 is triggered. p21 enforces the halt in the cycle dictated by p53 by binding to and inhibiting the activity of the Cdk/cyclin complexes." Removing p21 therefore breaks the effector step even though the p53 sensor is intact. (Choice A) is incorrect: p53 signals the halt but does not itself bind and inhibit the Cdk/cyclin complexes; that is p21''s job, so p53 acting "alone" cannot stall the cycle. (Choice B) is correct: with p21 knocked out, there is "no effective block on Cdk activation," so the cells cannot enforce the halt that p53 orders and instead proceed from G1 into S despite the damage. (Choice C) is incorrect: losing p21 removes the brake rather than making the arrest permanent; p21 opposes Cdk activation, so its absence releases the checkpoint rather than locking it shut. (Choice D) is incorrect: p53, not p21, is the multifunctional protein that detects damaged DNA at the G1 checkpoint, so p21 loss does not eliminate damage sensing. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the division of labor among p53 and p21 to predict how knocking out only the downstream effector changes checkpoint behavior, rather than recalling that p53 loss causes cancer.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b9cc365d-6c17-42bf-8035-7be772d035eb';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b9cc365d-6c17-42bf-8035-7be772d035eb';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b9cc365d-6c17-42bf-8035-7be772d035eb', 'A', 'misconception', 'attributes the downstream effector''s function to the upstream sensor'),
  ('b9cc365d-6c17-42bf-8035-7be772d035eb', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'treats loss of a brake as strengthening the arrest'),
  ('b9cc365d-6c17-42bf-8035-7be772d035eb', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses the effector (p21) with the damage sensor (p53)');

-- [Mitotic Structures] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During anaphase, a cell filmed under a microscope stretches from roughly spherical into an elongated oval as its separated chromosomes travel toward the poles. Which structure is MOST directly responsible for lengthening the whole cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Polar microtubules, which extend from opposite poles and overlap one another in the cell''s midzone"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Kinetochore microtubules, which extend from opposite poles and bind to the chromosomes at the centromere"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Astral microtubules, which radiate from each pole outward toward the nearby cell cortex"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Actin microfilaments, which assemble into a contractile ring at the former metaphase plate"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' The mitotic spindle contains functionally distinct microtubule populations, and the textbook notes that during anaphase the cell "becomes visibly elongated (oval shaped) as the polar microtubules slide against each other at the metaphase plate where they overlap." Because these fibers overlap in the middle of the cell and slide apart, they drive the two poles farther apart and stretch the whole cell, which must be distinguished from the fibers that move chromosomes and from the cytokinesis machinery. (Choice A) is correct: polar microtubules do not engage chromosomes; they overlap midway between the poles, and motor-driven sliding of these antiparallel fibers past one another pushes the poles apart, elongating the cell into an oval. (Choice B) is incorrect: kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes at the kinetochore and shorten to pull the separated chromatids toward the poles; they generate poleward chromosome movement, not the midzone sliding that lengthens the cell. (Choice C) is incorrect: astral microtubules radiate from the poles toward the cortex and function in positioning and orienting the spindle and regulating mitosis, not in the equatorial overlap-and-slide that elongates the cell. (Choice D) is incorrect: actin microfilaments form the contractile ring at the former metaphase plate during cytokinesis and pinch the cell inward to create the cleavage furrow; they constrict rather than lengthen the cell. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of antiparallel microtubule sliding to an observed phenotype and rule out three other real spindle and cytoskeletal populations that act at the same time but produce different movements.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b5acea84-0673-43e0-b461-950c1e8205fd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b5acea84-0673-43e0-b461-950c1e8205fd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b5acea84-0673-43e0-b461-950c1e8205fd', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates chromosome-moving fibers with cell-elongating fibers'),
  ('b5acea84-0673-43e0-b461-950c1e8205fd', 'C', 'misconception', 'overextends astral cortical-pulling role to whole-cell elongation'),
  ('b5acea84-0673-43e0-b461-950c1e8205fd', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses cytokinesis contractile machinery with anaphase spindle elongation');

-- [Loss of Cell-Cycle Control & Cancer] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A geneticist sequences a colorectal tumor and identifies a single point mutation affecting only one of the two copies of a gene encoding a growth-factor receptor. The mutation locks the receptor in a constantly "on" state, and the tumor cells divide continuously even in culture conditions where normal cells would stop. The other copy of the gene, and the cell''s other cell-cycle regulators, are unmutated. Based on how this mutation produces continuous division, the affected gene is best classified as:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "an oncogene, because a mutation that increases the gene product''s activity accelerates the cell cycle"}, {"label": "B", "text": "a tumor suppressor gene, because a mutation that eliminates the gene product''s activity accelerates the cell cycle"}, {"label": "C", "text": "a tumor suppressor gene, because the gene product normally signals surrounding cells to undergo apoptosis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "an oncogene, because the gene product normally halts the cycle until DNA damage is repaired"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' The textbook frames cell-cycle control like a car''s cruise control: proto-oncogene products (such as growth-factor receptors and signaling molecules) are the accelerator that pushes the cycle forward, while tumor suppressor gene products are the brake that sends stop signals. Control is lost in two ways: an overactive accelerator or an underactive brake. A gain-of-function mutation that converts a proto-oncogene into an oncogene makes an oncoprotein that drives division even when it is undesirable, whereas a tumor suppressor becomes dangerous when a loss-of-function mutation removes its stop signal. (Choice A) is correct: a growth-factor receptor is a proto-oncogene product, and a mutation that makes it constitutively active is a gain-of-function change that converts it to an oncogene, so the accelerator is stuck on and the cycle is driven forward. (Choice B) is incorrect: tumor suppressor genes cause cancer through loss of function, not a single activating mutation, and the data show an activating mutation that increases activity, which is the opposite of eliminating it. (Choice C) is incorrect: apoptotic and stop signals are functions of tumor suppressor products, but this gene''s mutation increases activity and drives division, so it cannot be acting as a brake; the classification contradicts the observed effect. (Choice D) is incorrect: halting the cycle until DNA is repaired is a tumor-suppressor (brake) function, so this description is mismatched to the oncogene label and to a mutation that accelerates division. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the accelerator/brake and gain-of-function/loss-of-function framework to the mutation data and infer the gene class from its effect rather than from a memorized label.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '43367870-e156-4f6c-b85c-85340ef3ceaf';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '43367870-e156-4f6c-b85c-85340ef3ceaf';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('43367870-e156-4f6c-b85c-85340ef3ceaf', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confusing gain-of-function (oncogene) with loss-of-function (tumor suppressor)'),
  ('43367870-e156-4f6c-b85c-85340ef3ceaf', 'C', 'misconception', 'attributing tumor-suppressor (apoptosis/stop) function to an activating mutation'),
  ('43367870-e156-4f6c-b85c-85340ef3ceaf', 'D', 'misconception', 'pairing the oncogene label with a brake/checkpoint function');

-- [Spermatogenesis] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher isolates a single primary spermatocyte from a seminiferous tubule and allows it to complete meiosis I only, arresting each resulting cell before meiosis II can begin. She then measures the chromosome content of one of these arrested daughter cells. Assuming a normal human karyotype, what does she find in that cell?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "23 chromosomes, each consisting of two sister chromatids"}, {"label": "B", "text": "23 chromosomes, each consisting of a single chromatid"}, {"label": "C", "text": "46 chromosomes, each consisting of two sister chromatids"}, {"label": "D", "text": "46 chromosomes, each consisting of a single chromatid"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gametogenesis & Reproductive Anatomy (BIO).'' A primary spermatocyte is diploid and has already replicated its DNA, so it enters meiosis I with 46 chromosomes, each made of two sister chromatids. OpenStax explains that during meiosis I "each of the 23 pairs of chromosomes separates," producing secondary spermatocytes "each with only half the number of chromosomes," and that the individual replicated chromosomes only divide later, during meiosis II. Meiosis I is a reductional division: it separates homologous partners but not sister chromatids, so chromosome number is halved while each chromosome remains a two-chromatid structure until meiosis II. (Choice A) is correct: after meiosis I the cell is a haploid secondary spermatocyte with 23 chromosomes, but because meiosis II has not yet occurred, each of those chromosomes still consists of two sister chromatids joined at the centromere. (Choice B) is incorrect: 23 single-chromatid chromosomes describe the state reached only after meiosis II, when sister chromatids separate to form spermatids; the cell here is arrested before meiosis II. (Choice C) is incorrect: 46 two-chromatid chromosomes describe the primary spermatocyte before meiosis I begins, but the homologous pairs have already separated, so the count cannot still be 46. (Choice D) is incorrect: 46 single-chromatid chromosomes describe an unreplicated diploid somatic cell; this mistakenly assumes meiosis I only reshuffles chromosomes without reducing their number and ignores that the DNA was replicated beforehand. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between reductional meiosis I (homolog separation, halving chromosome number) and equational meiosis II (sister-chromatid separation) to determine both the chromosome count and the chromatid state of a cell caught between the two divisions.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f888632a-3b07-48f1-aa5e-118c5dfafbc0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f888632a-3b07-48f1-aa5e-118c5dfafbc0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f888632a-3b07-48f1-aa5e-118c5dfafbc0', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates haploid chromosome number with completed sister-chromatid separation (skips ahead to the post-meiosis-II state)'),
  ('f888632a-3b07-48f1-aa5e-118c5dfafbc0', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes chromosome number is halved only at meiosis II, so counts the pre-meiosis-I state'),
  ('f888632a-3b07-48f1-aa5e-118c5dfafbc0', 'D', 'misconception', 'thinks meiosis I merely reshuffles chromosomes without reduction and forgets pre-meiotic DNA replication');

